The caller exists and is unscheduled — correcting the fitting's one remaining wrong claim
A third pass over `adoption/worked/artist-lockers.md`, correcting a claim that survived both earlier
ones and that changed a recommendation.

## The correction

Both passes said: *"`bot/cdn.py` exposes `is_expired()` and `expires_within()`, and nothing calls
either in aggregate."*

**Wrong.** `bot/refresh.py:69` calls `expires_within` while walking every tracked category's
`refs.json` — and it does more than count. It re-fetches the originating message for a fresh signed
URL and writes the file back. **The repair is written, complete, and better than the report we
recommended building.**

What is actually true is narrower and worse:

> `bin/refresh` exists and **nothing schedules it**. `bin/daily` is `init + sync + pin`; refresh is
> not in it and there is no other caller. The project can already fix its own link rot and only does
> when a person remembers.

## What that changes

Recommendation 2 was *"an advocate seat — because it is free and their first one writes itself."* It
is retitled **"a schedule — because the seat we proposed turned out to be the wrong shape."**

- **A seat that counts is no longer the cheapest real thing.** Proposing a report to somebody who has
  a repair is a downgrade. A seat here would have to ask a different question — *is the repair
  running, and did it keep up* — which is about a schedule, not about decay.
- **The cadence is the finding.** Measured from their own committed data, `ex − is` is exactly
  `86400`: every link dies 24 hours after issue. So `bin/daily`'s natural daily rhythm sits precisely
  *on* the link lifetime with no margin, and `refresh` is what buys the margin back. Nothing
  schedules either.

That is a binding problem, not a code problem, and it is answered by a `residency.yml` in their
repository declaring both verbs `kind: periodic` —
[DiscoveryWritten/artist-lockers#1](https://github.com/DiscoveryWritten/artist-lockers/pull/1),
written because of this fitting.

## The lesson, appended to finding 7

Finding 7 already said a fitting must read the artifact and promised *"which file and which line for
every claim it makes."* That promise was made **in the same document** as the wrong claim, and it did
not catch it:

> Citing a file and a line disciplines a **positive** claim and does nothing for a **negative** one.
> *This exists, here* is checked by reading one place; *nothing does this* is only checked by
> searching every place, and neither pass ran the search. **A negative claim is a grep you have not
> run yet** — and in a fitting it is the most dangerous kind, because every *"they have not built X"*
> is a recommendation to build X.

Three of the four corrections this fitting has now taken are the same error: telling them to build
something they already had.

## Also

The repository moved orgs. The attribution line now reads `DiscoveryWritten/artist-lockers`, noting
that `tiliv/artist-lockers` redirects. Same commit, `3f68225`, throughout — no re-fit.

## Deliberately not done

- **No new recommendations.** This narrows one and corrects the record; the ranking is unchanged.
- **`refresh` was not wired into `daily`** in their repository. It is a one-line change and a
  behaviour change, so it wants its own PR and its own argument — not a drive-by from a fitting.
